@page "/"

<PageTitle>wpflow · Plug in. Adjust. Watch.</PageTitle>

<div class="home">
    <section class="hero">
        <div>
            <h1>Plug in. Adjust. <span>Watch.</span></h1>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-side">
            <p>The node-based graph editor for WPF and Blazor. Unplug a cable and the render reacts immediately.</p>
            <a class="button" href="exemples">Browse the examples →</a>
            <a class="button quiet" href="doc">Read the documentation</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <HeroFlow />

    <p class="hero-note">
        This graph is a real Blazor <code>FlowView</code> running in your browser, in WebAssembly. Select
        a cable then press <kbd>Delete</kbd>; drag a new wire between the same two points to plug it back in.
    </p>

    <section class="packages" aria-label="Packages">
        <article>
            <h2>Wpflow.Blazor</h2>
            <p>For Blazor Server and WebAssembly. This is the version this site runs.</p>
            <pre><code>dotnet add package Wpflow.Blazor</code></pre>
            <a href="doc/blazor">Blazor guide →</a>
        </article>
        <article>
            <h2>Wpflow.Wpf</h2>
            <p>WPF controls, with the same gestures and events. Windows only: nothing to try here.</p>
            <pre><code>dotnet add package Wpflow.Wpf</code></pre>
            <a href="doc/learn/01-quickstart">WPF quick start →</a>
        </article>
        <article>
            <h2>Wpflow.Core</h2>
            <p>The shared core, without UI: immutable model, changes, edge paths, JSON, layout.</p>
            <pre><code>dotnet add package Wpflow.Core</code></pre>
            <a href="doc/reference/utils">Utilities →</a>
        </article>
    </section>

    <footer>
        <p>
            License
            <a href="@($"{DocLibrary.Repository}/blob/main/LICENSE")">MIT</a>.
        </p>
    </footer>
</div>
